- Be ridiculous to say
- It's economic then,

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.610
is the reason that
causes these disparities?

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.040
- Economic, but I don't
think it's related to color.

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.420
- But would you say if there's a majority

00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:59.790
of a particular group that lies underneath

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:01.080
the poverty line in economic,

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.700
that has race has
anything to do with that,

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.245
is what you're saying?
- It could, it could.

00:19:04.245 --> 00:19:06.360
But it's just not blanket.

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:07.560
You don't know.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.530
Why don't we look at the
families, the Black families,

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:14.190
who have done those amazing
things in this country

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:16.920
and study what they did to succeed

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.290
instead of just focusing on,

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:21.120
oh, well, Black people are behind

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:22.260
and we don't even know what to do.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:23.580
Why don't we just go and look?

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:25.020
Since you are a professor,

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.910
wouldn't that be you go and
look at what has worked-

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:29.100
- Well, I would never just go
to you and just look at you.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.450
I, again, and I'm sorry.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:31.853
It's just the reality of the data.

00:19:31.853 --> 00:19:32.834
And so what I show-

00:19:32.834 --> 00:19:34.590
- Data can be broken umpteen

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:35.423
different ways.
- Then you are

00:19:35.423 --> 00:19:36.256
choosing your data.
- No, no it couldn't.

00:19:36.256 --> 00:19:37.089
- Yes it can.

00:19:37.089 --> 00:19:37.922
- No, actually, I just give the data.

00:19:37.922 --> 00:19:38.755
- You're a professor
and you're saying that-

00:19:38.755 --> 00:19:39.588
- That's why I give data-
- Data cannot be manipulated?

00:19:39.588 --> 00:19:40.650
- I give, I don't manipulate data.

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:43.020
- This is why my kids do not
go to public school anymore.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:44.340
- I don't and I'm glad they don't.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.210
- I just think it's interesting.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:47.100
I'm thinking of like an
anecdotal story I have.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.560
Last year the school that I
taught at, deep East Oakland.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.960
I serve majority Black and Brown students.

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:54.540
Over 70% of our families
under the poverty line.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:56.160
These are some of the
hardest working people

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:57.930
I have ever seen in my life.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.820
They get up, they have two or three jobs,

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:00.780
three or four children.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:01.987
- Because of, I would argue-

00:20:01.987 --> 00:20:04.100
- Because of their economic status, right?

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:06.390
- No because of liberal policies,

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.490
that is the government telling them

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:09.870
that they can and cannot succeed.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.210
Because of how higher
property taxes are here.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:13.560
Because we do not have school choice

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:15.540
where they can take their
dollars somewhere else.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:17.190
If you put those same families

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:18.990
in redder districts in the country,

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:21.870
like places in Florida,
and Texas, or Virginia

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:24.030
where they have better
options for their family,

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:25.858
you would definitely see them be able to

00:20:25.858 --> 00:20:26.691
succeed in different-
- And where Black

00:20:26.691 --> 00:20:28.680
and Brown children are still
underperforming in schools?

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.960
- But coming from Florida,
the school that I was at,

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.170
it's graded from like A to like D, F.

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:36.330
When I started there, it was a C school.

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:37.770
Very poor performing.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:39.870
And technically, sure,
they have school choice.

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:41.280
They don't have state tests anymore,

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:44.040
but they're still so badly performing.

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:46.830
The community of it, Black, Brown,

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:50.130
very little White students
in that community.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.980
But not to say that student of
color can't be more affluent,

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:54.930
but I think it's ridiculous to kind of say

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.360
that color doesn't play a part

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:01.470
into disparities and just disadvantages.

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:05.820
So in Florida a lot of
people that I had encountered

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:08.160
weren't kind to others

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:12.243
and sometimes I would have
coworkers call students,

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:16.710
and using racist slurs
and things like that.

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:20.070
And from my standpoint,
hearing something like that,

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:21.300
it disgusted me.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.920
My coworkers very quickly were able

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:25.590
to kind of distinguish me from the rest.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:28.170
Like if I was gonna come
to the teacher's lounge,

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:30.030
more hush conversation.

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:32.640
People wouldn't share
as much as they used to,

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.761
just 'cause I would call
out behavior like that.

00:21:34.761 --> 00:21:35.763
(object thwacks softly)

00:21:35.763 --> 00:21:37.183
- [Ragini] Teachers with
guns make schools safer.

00:21:40.476 --> 00:21:43.893
(footfalls thud loudly)

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:48.134
- [Shaun] You know what,
I'm gonna disagree.

00:21:48.134 --> 00:21:48.990
(Kenyon laughs loudly)

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.910
- So I was really in between,

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:53.670
I didn't really know how I
felt about this issue at first.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:56.190
But then I started looking at places

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:58.410
that had armed their teachers.

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:00.450
So I believe that model is good.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:01.740
It has to be voluntary.

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:02.573
- Yes.
- So the teachers

00:22:02.573 --> 00:22:04.110
have to want to do it themselves.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.640
They have to be well trained.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:07.200
- Yes.
- They have to have

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.270
background checks periodically.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:10.740
That has been successful.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:12.150
- If we're trusting teachers,

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:14.310
which, in some cases, we
do, in some cases, we don't.

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:17.010
But my mindset is that if
we're gonna trust teachers

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:19.530
to mold our children's
minds and educate them,

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:21.390
they're there for eight hours a day,

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:24.120
we should also trust
and kind of enable them

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:26.310
to protect themselves
and our kids physically.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:27.360
- I think by no means should

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.760
we ever force a teacher to carry a gun.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:33.390
But if a teacher is willing
to take on the responsibility

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.600
of learning how to
handle a weapon properly,

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.270
I think that we should allow
them to do that for us.

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.722
And it, frankly, would be
an incredible service to us.

00:22:41.722 --> 00:22:45.055
(object thwacks softly)

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:48.450
- All right, so just to break it open,

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:50.069
this conversation is so much more nuanced

00:22:50.069 --> 00:22:52.140
than to arm or not to arm.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:53.880
And if you want to save a school,

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.590
you need to get parental engagement.

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:57.540
Let's look at the mindset
of the individuals

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:59.850
that are going into these
homes with firearms.

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:01.950
Those individuals are not mentally okay,

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:03.570
there's something wrong happening

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:05.190
whether it's at school, in the house,

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:06.060
whether it's, who knows,

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:07.860
maybe it's something
they're consuming online.

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:11.640
And I think that's the parents'
job to continue to engage

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:13.290
to make sure that child is healthy.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.630
A lot of these kids as well
that are going into schools

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:17.970
that are not mentally healthy,

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:19.680
they're coming from
single parent households,

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.480
they're coming, they're living
with their grandparents.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:23.970
There's something intrinsically

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:25.200
with their identity that's broken.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.360
And I think that whether you
arm a school, or you don't,

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:28.815
it's not gonna change
it if you don't address.

00:23:28.815 --> 00:23:29.648
- It's like a harsh-

00:23:29.648 --> 00:23:30.990
- I agree with you, the
people that are, you know,

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:34.470
perpetrating these crimes and
these like mass casualties

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:35.850
are messed up individuals.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.440
I would absolutely agree.

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:39.660
Mass shootings are a
uniquely American problem.

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:41.250
So if we try to understand
what's the root,

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:43.590
why does America solely have that problem,

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:45.270
I would say it's a gun culture problem.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:46.500
So if the idea is, you know,

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:49.170
should we try to remove
aspects of gun culture

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:52.710
that really pervade in our
society, should we do that?

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.840
Or should we just increase
the amount of firearms

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.550
we can have in every single space

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:57.840
just so that way we're properly ready

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:58.680
for a gun fight whenever it comes up?

00:23:58.680 --> 00:23:59.880
- I wanna clarify gun culture.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:01.050
Are you saying that there's like

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:02.520
the shoot 'em up, bang
'em up, video games,

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:03.353
are you saying the individuals-

00:24:03.353 --> 00:24:04.186
- No, I wouldn't say it's video games.

00:24:04.186 --> 00:24:05.760
I'd say institutions like the NRA

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:07.080
that heavily impose, you know,

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:08.610
you need to own, not just firearms,

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:09.986
but as many firearms as you can.

00:24:09.986 --> 00:24:10.819
(air whooshing loudly)

00:24:10.819 --> 00:24:12.630
I personally would not like to be armed.

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.940
I do not want to take lethal
action in the classroom

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:17.850
and I want to make it a
safe, inclusive environment.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:22.850
Arming teachers is a response
measure to particular violence

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:25.650
that goes on in our
schools here in America.

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:27.780
And personally, I think
we should focus more

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:29.520
towards solutions to prevent

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:31.050
these occurrences from happening,

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:33.300
rather than just implementing responses,

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:35.432
implying that these actions will occur.

00:24:35.432 --> 00:24:37.110
- And I'm just gonna be honest with you,

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:38.580
you don't want me to have a gun,

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:41.850
because I had to go back to
therapy in the last few months.

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:44.070
And so I agree with a lot of
what you guys were saying,

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:45.060
what your plan was.

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.250
But I would also say mental health.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:49.320
We gotta understand some of
these school shootings are done

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:50.640
because people have snapped.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:53.010
- People have natural
biases and things like that

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:54.780
and people could act on those biases,

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:57.210
then they could be guns drawn

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:59.940
and not a clear mind, you know.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:01.620
As a parent, how would you feel knowing

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:04.140
that you don't know
which teachers have a gun

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:06.210
and you've had bad experiences
with prior teachers?

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:09.465
What if that teacher that
absolutely, you know,

00:25:09.465 --> 00:25:10.754
blown up-
- So my answer.

00:25:10.754 --> 00:25:11.587
- Kinda thing.
- My answer,

00:25:11.587 --> 00:25:13.500
my answer to your question
is I feel the same way

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:15.540
about the guard in front
of my kid's school.

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:16.890
I don't really know the guard,

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:18.660
but he's gone through training

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:20.640
and I presume that he
knows what he's doing.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:22.830
And frankly, I feel the same
way about a police officer.

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:24.120
I don't know the police officer,

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:25.290
but they've gone through training

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.420
and they were willing to
take on that responsibility.

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:29.250
And so I would apply the same logic.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.900
It's so interesting that you bring that up

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:32.910
because it really ties back to why I feel

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:34.020
like we need to be careful

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:37.050
about how much responsibility
we're saddling teachers

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:38.850
with teaching our kids values, right.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:40.800
On the one hand, the system is saying,

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:43.260
trust our teachers, they
know better than you parents.

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:44.370
We should be able to teach them

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.380
what they need to know about sexuality.

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.030
On the other hand, you guys are admitting

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:53.030
that many teachers are not
really checked for mental issues.

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:55.620
We don't really know what is
happening in these classrooms.

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:56.453
We don't know-
- I'm not saying that

00:25:56.453 --> 00:25:57.720
teachers are not checked.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:00.900
We very frequently have like
professional development days

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:02.340
where we got self care.

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:04.620
So I wouldn't say many, by any means.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:06.540
And when I'm coming into the workplace,

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:07.920
I'm not bringing in all my baggage,

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:10.020
as to say, you know,
you go into your work,

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.250
you're not like, oh, I'm so depressed,

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:12.083
my kid's this, this, that.

00:26:12.083 --> 00:26:13.650
- So why, if there is a teacher

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.480
who is willing to step forward

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:17.130
and will go through the training,

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:19.020
and will go through the screening,

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:21.840
why not empower them to save our kids?

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.210
I mean in Uvalde, if there was a teacher

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:25.890
who could actually save those children,

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:28.530
then maybe we would not
have seen so much death.

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:31.890
- When we place teachers with
a firearm in the classroom,

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.410
it takes the instance
of a violent occurrence

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:36.450
from a possibility in the
minds of these children

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:37.766
to an inevitability in the minds

00:26:37.766 --> 00:26:38.730
of these children.
- But the children don't know.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.620
It is a concealed and carry weapon

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:42.180
that has gone through the process

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:43.710
of the local sheriff's department,

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:45.630
background check, training, checkups.

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:46.463
- [Quisha] Yeah, no one knows.

00:26:46.463 --> 00:26:47.296
- Everything.
- But the teacher-

00:26:47.296 --> 00:26:49.247
- No one knows.
- And the police department.

00:26:49.247 --> 00:26:50.730
- I have a logistical question.

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:51.780
- Yeah.
- Does this teacher

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:52.800
have the gun on them?

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:53.633
- Yes.
- You wouldn't know.

00:26:53.633 --> 00:26:54.570
- It is holstered on them?

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:55.403
- At all times.

00:26:55.403 --> 00:26:56.340
- [Marissa] It's a concealed carry.

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:57.173
- It should never be-
- So just to break down-

00:26:57.173 --> 00:26:58.313
- In a place of a child.
- What a concealed carry is.

00:26:58.313 --> 00:26:59.391
It's illegal to brandish-
- No, I know what

00:26:59.391 --> 00:27:00.224
a concealed carry is but-

00:27:00.224 --> 00:27:01.057
- I just wanna clarify.

00:27:01.057 --> 00:27:05.190
- My thing and why this
question sort of enrages me.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.560
Why are we asking teachers to carry guns?

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:09.780
When did this become
an occupational hazard?

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:11.790
When did we walk into
school and people were like,

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.290
by the way, you could be shot today,

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:15.210
so maybe you wanna have
your Glock strapped on you

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:17.520
because who knows, this might
be your last day on Earth.

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:18.510
That is ridiculous.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:20.490
If you 50 years ago were
to look at a teacher

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:23.430
and be like, hey, do you
wanna hold this gun with you,

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:25.050
you know, like you might need it.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:26.640
They're gonna scratch their
heads and look at you funny.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:28.740
This is opening up a
bigger conversation, right.

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:31.170
But I think that even though
it is voluntary, right,

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:32.760
we still need to have this conversation.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:34.890
Like it still needs to happen of why,

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:38.340
even if a teacher was willing
to carry a gun to school,

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:40.140
they are even having to be asked

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:41.265
to do that in the first place.

00:27:41.265 --> 00:27:42.420
(object thwacks softly)

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:45.296
- [Ragini] Teachers are not paid enough.

00:27:45.296 --> 00:27:48.629
(footfalls thud loudly)

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:54.180
- Every day I wake up
and I hand to you guys

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:57.540
the most precious thing in
my entire life, my children.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:59.880
I think that it's
important that our society

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.190
will reflect how much we value you guys.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:04.140
I think that you should receive bonuses

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:05.430
if you do a great job.

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:08.400
If you're able to represent
all of us as Americans,

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:10.800
both conservatives and liberals,

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.480
and you're able to speak on our behalf

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:13.620
and take care of our children,

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:14.940
we should reward you for that.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:16.347
And you should feel rewarded for that.

00:28:16.347 --> 00:28:18.720
And so, I don't know
exactly what you make,

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:20.850
but I think it is really important

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:24.210
that we invest in you
and we pour into you.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:26.490
And when I see the entire budget

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:27.630
that the state of California,

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.460
for example, spends on education

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:32.280
and I realize that a sliver fraction of it

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.770
only goes to you guys, it breaks my heart

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:36.690
and frankly, makes me so angry.

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:39.793
I don't need more bureaucrats,
I need more good teachers.

00:28:39.793 --> 00:28:41.460
- I left the K though 12

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:42.540
and went to the community college

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:44.700
and I do pretty well.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:45.930
So I'm gonna be clear with that.

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:46.826
But also I have a master's.

00:28:46.826 --> 00:28:48.030
- So you're not oppressed, thank God.

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:49.320
- So I have a master,

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:51.300
but I had to work really
hard to get where I'm at.

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:52.133
- Of course.

00:28:52.133 --> 00:28:53.730
- But I'm also in a doctoral program

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:55.500
and you're right, I'm
paying you outta pocket too.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:56.970
Again, I had a student
the other day saying,

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:58.350
I can't pay my electric bill.

00:28:58.350 --> 00:28:59.400
How much is your electric bill?

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:00.690
I'll pay your electric bill.

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:02.940
And so I put up students in hotels

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.560
because they have nowhere to live.

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:07.320
I have a student who
doesn't have a family.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.320
You know, he told me the other
day, I want, I need a dad.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:11.520
I said, I'll be your father,

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:13.590
because it just broke my
heart that a 19-year-old

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:16.320
is in this world alone,
doesn't have family.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.030
- Yeah, I would be
scared to like calculate

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:19.978
how much I spent for my classroom.

00:29:19.978 --> 00:29:20.811
- Yes.
- Right?

00:29:20.811 --> 00:29:22.170
like all the materials, all
the things that I'm purchasing,

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:23.970
all the things that I'm doing.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:26.580
And it's just interesting to think

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:29.820
that we are always told
like teachers are heroes.

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:31.650
They're teaching the youth of America.

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:33.750
But also we're not gonna
give you enough money

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:35.160
to survive comfortably.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:36.480
Like you're gonna struggle every day.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:37.920
There have been days when I go to work

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:39.810
and I'm like, I'm not gonna
have money to put gas in my car.

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:41.730
I guess we're gonna find out, right.

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:43.500
And I know so many other teachers

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:45.690
who are struggling to keep afloat.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:47.070
I can't even imagine having a,

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:49.260
I can't even think about
having a family as a teacher.

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:52.560
I think that growing up I was
not the strongest student.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.020
I didn't necessarily see the
point in what we were doing.

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:56.940
And I think that, ultimately,
that felt like it was

00:29:56.940 --> 00:30:00.120
because education was something
that I had to get through,

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.520
not necessarily something
that I was obtaining.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:04.890
But I think what really
pushed me to go into teaching

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:07.440
was wanting to give kids a space

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:10.290
where they didn't feel
the way I feel growing up.

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:11.850
Where they felt like they had power

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:13.410
to make choice in their education.

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.810
And that their education could be powerful

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:17.431
in aiding them to do
what they wanted to do.

00:30:17.431 --> 00:30:18.264
(object thwacks softly)

00:30:18.264 --> 00:30:20.475
(footfalls thud softly)

00:30:20.475 --> 00:30:22.050
(person speaking away from mic)

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:23.010
- So I'm not a teacher,

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:24.420
I dunno how much you guys make.

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:26.370
And I'm not gonna use you as the example,

00:30:26.370 --> 00:30:27.360
I'm gonna use a friend of mine.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:29.700
She's been at a school
for a number of years,

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:31.380
I wanna say it's six, seven, eight years.

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:32.520
And she's become tenured.

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:33.810
And through her tenureship,

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:35.460
she's accumulated so much wealth,

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:37.320
I'm appalled at how much she's making

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:39.960
'cause she teaches
Spanish to sixth graders

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:43.260
and she's making over $150,000 a year.

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:44.093
- Can I ask you a question?

00:30:44.093 --> 00:30:45.240
What kinda school does she work at?

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:46.073
- Public school.

00:30:46.073 --> 00:30:47.247
- Interesting.
- Yeah.

00:30:47.247 --> 00:30:48.150
- And so for me-

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:49.950
- I mean, how much experience
goes on with that too?

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:50.970
- It must, I'm assuming.

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:51.803
- Yeah.
- Because you've invested

00:30:51.803 --> 00:30:53.340
into your career, which
think everybody should.

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:54.360
But for me I look at it

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:57.390
as you're making $150,000 in nine months.

00:30:57.390 --> 00:31:00.150
- The prompt very clearly
was are teachers paid enough?

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:00.983
- [Shaun] Yes.

00:31:00.983 --> 00:31:02.430
- And I mean we're not,

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:04.920
I've had to work second
jobs doing DoorDash

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:06.990
just 'cause I wasn't making enough.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:09.270
If you look at these
shoes, they are ripped.

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:10.260
They are broken.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.180
From a teacher wishlist.

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.512
I could not even buy these shoes myself.

00:31:14.512 --> 00:31:15.345
You know what I'm saying?

00:31:15.345 --> 00:31:16.950
- Sure.
- I don't make enough money.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:17.850
- So I wanted to clarify.

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:18.840
This isn't an absolute,

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:20.640
I'm not saying that every
teacher is paid enough.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:21.840
I have no idea what your salary is.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:23.370
I have no idea any of those details.

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:26.160
I'm just recognizing that if a
teacher's getting nine months

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.620
and their salary's in nine
months, they get a summer off.

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:30.510
Most of society isn't given three months

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:31.343
to just go on vacation.
- That's assuming

00:31:31.343 --> 00:31:32.190
they have three months.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:34.500
I'm an 11 month employee
for one whole month

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:36.450
out of the year, I'm not paid anything.

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:37.500
- Same.
- Nothing.

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:39.270
I have to figure out my funds,

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:41.670
save money that I don't have

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:44.610
to try to supply for a
month, month and a half.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.880
I don't get supplementary income.

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:48.760
Like that doesn't happen.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:49.800
(object thwacks softly)

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:52.643
- [Ragini] Critical race
theory is anti-American.

00:31:52.643 --> 00:31:55.976
(footfalls thud softly)

00:31:57.660 --> 00:31:59.040
- Well, I'll go first.

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:02.130
My daughter, same teacher, actually,

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.350
who was asking about her pronouns,

00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:06.000
she said that she was gonna be introducing

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.290
